{"reference":"Romans 4","book":"Romans","chapter":4,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Romans 4 develops its argument through Abraham’s story rather than through a detached slogan about “faith versus works.” Abraham’s trust, his uncircumcised status, the promise, and God’s power to give life all belong to the chapter’s line of reasoning. Reading these features together keeps the focus on the scriptural narrative Paul is using.","verse_refs":["Romans 4:1-25"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_004_65dcf2f20dc61c6b333678f6"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Circumcision, Abraham, and Belonging","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Paul places Abraham’s trust before his later circumcision and calls him the father both of uncircumcised believers and of circumcised people who follow that trust. This presents gentile inclusion without treating Jewish circumcision as worthless, or allowing gentile believers to claim Abraham exclusively.","verse_refs":["Romans 4:1-12","Romans 4:9-17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_004_1347d8705943bd9ff8383c6e"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"The Promise and the Law","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"In verses 13–25, the promise is said to come through the righteousness of faith rather than through Torah, and Abraham’s trust is directed toward the God who gives life to the dead. The contrast should not be read as defining Judaism as graceless achievement or Torah itself as evil.","verse_refs":["Romans 4:13-25"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_004_d01767777328bc38e0e767af"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Romans in Context","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Romans is a letter addressed to particular circumstances, even though later theological systems have engaged it. Its arguments should therefore not be treated as a contextless outline, nor should its historical analogies automatically be mapped onto modern nations, groups, parties, or institutions.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_romans_004_be5760f179e6d90c32d080ea"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}